gm-method (compact + parity-safe quantization of a baked field via a SPATIAL INDEX of per-sector bounds): to shrink a baked height/data grid while keeping resolution everywhere AND keeping two readers (e.g. CPU collider + GPU render) in agreement, partition the grid into SxS-node SECTORS, store each sector's own [min,max], and pack each node as a B-bit int NORMALIZED to its sector's range. Flat sectors get fine resolution (small range / 2^B levels); steep sectors spend their bits on their large range -> high EFFECTIVE resolution everywhere at low bit-depth. Measured on a real GPU-baked terrain heightfield: S=8nodes/8bit gave maxErr ~0.1m at ~50% the flat-float JSON size; 16-bit was ~lossless at ~78%. KEY parity property: both readers consume the SAME shared sector bounds + ints (shared baked data, NOT independent float recompute) so they do NOT diverge - this is why output-quant of INDEPENDENT recomputes fails (boundary straddles) but sector-bounded quant of a SHARED bake succeeds. SEAM-FREE: dequantize the whole grid to one flat array at load (a border node lands in exactly one sector; bilinear across the boundary blends two correctly-dequantized neighbors -> sub-quantum smoothness, no visible step). Loader: branch on an artifact.sectors field, expand to Float32 once, keep the existing bilinear path. Reusable for veg/rock instance buffers (per-chunk bounds) + any large float buffer where small absolute error is acceptable. JSON int-arrays aren't maximally compact; a binary/typed-array artifact is ~1B/node for the real win if size dominates.